title: Unsafe code
tags: Development
kind: reference
summary: Where the code is unsafe and why: only what cannot be otherwise — between fork and exec, the LMDB map, fanotify — each block saying why it is sound, and system calls through rustix rather than libc.
audience: dev

An `unsafe` block is where Rust stops checking and the code vouches for itself. The
project keeps them to what cannot be written otherwise, and makes each one say why
it is sound.

!! The rules

* ''A system call goes through `rustix`'', which wraps it safely (typed descriptors, a `Result`), or through `std` where it has the call (`File::try_lock` is the store's `flock`). `libc` is for what `rustix` cannot do safely: the calls between `fork` and `exec`, setting a signal disposition, and what `rustix` does not wrap — which the broker gathers in `watchd/src/sys.rs`, one safe function each.
* ''Every block says why it is sound'', in a `// SAFETY:` comment just above it. Clippy's `undocumented_unsafe_blocks`, set for the workspace in the root `Cargo.toml`, fails [[Static checks]] on one that does not.
* ''A crate with no unsafe code forbids it'' (`#![forbid(unsafe_code)]`): the CLI, the bench, the query oracle. `core` denies it, and allows it at its one exception.

!! What is left, and why

|!Where |!What |!Why it cannot be safe |
|`gui` `sandbox.rs`, `shell_session.rs` |the `pre_exec` hooks |the closure runs in the child between `fork` and `exec`, where only async-signal-safe calls are allowed — no allocation, no lock — and the compiler cannot check that |
|`gui` `lib.rs`, `sandbox.rs` |`std::env::set_var` |another thread could read the environment meanwhile; done at startup, before any |
|`daemon` `kvstore/`, `sync.rs`; `core` `trash.rs` |opening and resizing an LMDB map |the file is mapped in memory: it must be opened once per process, and nothing may write to it behind the map ([[Storage safety]]) |
|`daemon` `kvstore/resident.rs` |`mlock`, `munlock`, `madvise`, reading the pages |they take raw addresses, which must be a live part of the map ([[Holding a repository in memory]]) |
|`watchd` `sys.rs` |fanotify, file handles, a filesystem's fsid, a socket peer's credentials |`rustix` has no fanotify nor file handles, keeps an fsid's two ints private, and cannot hold the pid 0 of a peer in another pid namespace. The events themselves are parsed from bytes, safely |

Tests open a store's file raw to damage it or set it back to an older format
(`common::kv::raw_env` in the daemon's tests); two set a signal disposition, and the
broker's interrupts a thread with a signal to test what an EINTR does.

The handle half of `sys.rs` (`open_by_handle_at`) runs only where the kernel lets a
handle be resolved: not in a container whose seccomp profile refuses it, where its
tests check that nothing is reported instead ([[Testing the watcher]]).

!! What `unsafe` does not cover

A segmentation fault of the GUI is not necessarily ours: WebKit and GTK are C and C++
code in the same process. Read the core dump's stack before suspecting a block
here — the one the GUI met in October 2026 was a WebKitGTK bug, a paste while the
clipboard's owner did not answer.
